  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The performance and cost-saving claims are based on computational experiments rather than a deployment in a real-world logistics network.",
      "what": "This study models and solves two stochastic variants of the vehicle routing problem with roaming customers under joint delivery, accounting for either stochastic travel times or stochastic time windows. The problem is formulated as a two-stage stochastic model with recourse and solved using an adaptive large neighborhood search heuristic combined with a late acceptance hill-climbing strategy. Computational experiments compare two sampling strategies, showing that Sample Average Approximation outperforms Fixed Sample Size sampling in reducing costs for large-sized instances, while both perform similarly on small and medium instances.",
      "why": "It introduces a optimization framework for joint delivery systems that can handle the real-world uncertainty of both customer locations and travel times, demonstrating that accounting for these stochastic elements yields significant operational cost savings."
    }
  },
